1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,834
How this course works.

2
00:00:01,834 --> 00:00:07,794
Use each lesson as a two-window study session: explanation here, analysis in Neurodesk.

3
00:00:07,794 --> 00:00:12,379
The goal is clear: Set up an effective two-window workspace.

4
00:00:13,229 --> 00:00:14,501
Understand, observe, reproduce.

5
00:00:14,501 --> 00:00:22,551
Each workflow is split into a concept, a real interface walkthrough, an independent Neurodesk task, and an output check.

6
00:00:22,551 --> 00:00:30,602
The point is not to memorize button positions; it is to understand what information enters and leaves each step.

7
00:00:30,602 --> 00:00:31,449
Pause often.

8
00:00:31,449 --> 00:00:36,957
Predict what a command or FEAT setting will do before you run it.

9
00:00:36,957 --> 00:00:43,313
Then compare the result with the expected output and explain discrepancies in your own words.

10
00:00:44,163 --> 00:00:47,575
Now connect the idea to an observable check.

11
00:00:47,575 --> 00:00:51,839
First, place this lesson on one side of your screen.

12
00:00:51,839 --> 00:00:54,824
Next, place Neurodesk on the other side.

13
00:00:54,824 --> 00:00:59,089
Then, create a notes file for observations and unresolved questions.

14
00:00:59,089 --> 00:01:02,927
Record what you observe so the decision remains reviewable.

15
00:01:03,777 --> 00:01:06,801
Practical labs work best on a computer.

16
00:01:06,801 --> 00:01:15,011
Videos and reading work on mobile, but use a desktop or laptop for Neurodesk exercises and side-by-side quality control.

17
00:01:15,011 --> 00:01:20,197
A useful self-check is this: When should you look at expected output?

18
00:01:20,197 --> 00:01:27,542
After making a prediction and running the step yourself, so the comparison tests understanding rather than copying.

19
00:01:28,392 --> 00:01:32,097
To recap: First, predict before running a step.

20
00:01:32,097 --> 00:01:37,654
Next, record what you observe, especially when it differs from the example.

21
00:01:37,654 --> 00:01:44,600
Before continuing, make sure you can explain this objective: Set up an effective two-window workspace.
